#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "865035a9-dfe6-51bf-a5dc-624240267541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087097} {:answer/id #uuid "981d3229-34da-5547-9622-76e7a57b9b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087098} {:answer/id #uuid "ff08f834-c93c-58b6-a6eb-be4ab302b1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087099} {:answer/id #uuid "5826ab39-99cb-5f0d-a91a-c8e47d4e7431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087100} {:answer/id #uuid "58fc334c-401e-58ca-8084-7bbee1c81411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "865035a9-dfe6-51bf-a5dc-624240267541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087097} :answer-field/id #uuid "212166f2-0485-5ec6-aaa6-538998150651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9a96319-3bb3-5dd4-a3ac-27f5effab07d" :question/math-academy-id "q-8331" :question/problem "Let\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $T(kv) = kT(v)$ for any real $2 \\cdot 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n2. $T(v_{1} + v_{2}) = T(v_{1}) + T(v_{2})$ for any real $2 \\cdot 1$ column vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$\n3. $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}ky \\\\ kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}]}
